# Ctrl-Z on a running *internal* job (crush's own builtin `sleep`, not `cmd`-wrapped)
# must suspend it and return control to the nested crush session's own prompt, with
# the job showing up as Paused -- same as tests/pty_ctrl_z_suspends_external_job.crush,
# but exercising job-control signal delivery against a builtin command instead of a
# forked child process.
#
# `pty_send_control` (see pty_test_helpers.crush) sends the Ctrl-Z byte and resends it
# if it doesn't land rather than waiting out a fixed guess beforehand -- see that
# file's own doc comment, and `pty_ctrl_z_suspends_external_job.crush`'s, for why a
# fixed settle delay can't actually guarantee this (the kernel generates SIGTSTP for
# whichever process owns the terminal independent of whether crush has dispatched the
# job yet) and why resending is the correct fix instead of a longer guess.
source tests/pty_test_helpers.crush
$t := $(pty_spawn)
$n := $(pty_enable_counted_prompt $t)

$t:write "sleep 100s\n"
$n = $(pty_send_control $t $n $pty:control:z)
pty_assert_job_status $t 0 "Paused"
$t:close
